    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that sounds solid. i'm a bit worried about the kitchen staff actually adhering to it though, especially during the rush. do you have any advice on how to create a checklist or a protocol for the line cooks to make sure they aren't cross-contaminating or just ignoring the red font during the dinner service?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To ensure adherence during the rush, you need a system that is as rigid as the protocol you've established for the planning phase. Implement a \"Two-Person Verification\" system for all allergy-sensitive items, where a line cook prepares the dish and a designated expediter or supervisor visually confirms the preparation against the allergy manifest before it leaves the line. This is non-negotiable, much like a quality control check on a line of highly valuable, albeit small, pieces of evidence.\n\nFurthermore, dedicate specific, color-coded prep tools and cutting boards exclusively for allergen-free items—think of them as segregated zones. You can even institute a brief, mandatory \"Allergy Huddle\" meeting right before service begins, where the Chef reviews the most critical allergies one last time. This small ritual keeps the high-risk items top-of-mind, preventing the chaos of the dinner rush from causing a lapse in attention.",
      "source": "target",
